  {
    "id": "pfister-stellen",
    "name": "Pfister Stellen LG529 Pull-Down Kitchen Faucet",
    "badge": "Best Warranty",
    "category": "Pull-Down, Single Handle, 1 or 3 Hole",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "description": "The Stellen is the faucet to buy if you plan to stay in the house and want the warranty to stay with you. Pfister rates the Stellen LG529 line at 1.8 gpm, and supplier listings give a total height of 16-3/8 inches, a high-arc spout and a maximum deck thickness of 3 inches, the most generous of our picks for thick stone counters. The pull-down head offers stream, spray and a pause control. The real draw is Pfister's Pforever Warranty, which the company describes as covering finish and function for as long as you own your home, with proof of purchase or original ownership required for a claim. Pfister is less common in plumbing supply houses than Delta and Moen, so parts may be easier to order than to buy over the counter. Several Stellen suffixes exist across finishes, and some retailers sell near-identical models under other numbers.",
    "pros": [
      "1.8 gpm rated flow, per Pfister",
      "Fits decks up to 3 in thick",
      "Pforever warranty covers finish and function for life",
      "Pause control on the spray head"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Parts less common at supply houses than Delta or Moen",
      "No magnetic docking listed",
      "Several near-identical model numbers"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The Pfister Stellen is the value-and-warranty pick: 1.8 gpm, a high arc for tall pots and lifetime finish and function coverage."
  },
  {
    "id": "american-standard-colony-pro",
    "name": "American Standard Colony PRO 7077300 Pull-Down Kitchen Faucet",
    "badge": "Best Budget",
    "category": "Pull-Down, Single Handle, Single Hole",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "description": "The Colony PRO is the pick when you need a dependable faucet for a rental, a laundry sink or a quick kitchen refresh without paying for touch electronics or magnetic docking. American Standard rates the current 7077300 models at 1.5 gpm and describes a dual-spray pull-down head, and retailers list a ceramic disc valve and a limited lifetime warranty on function and finish. It is a plain, conventional design that will look at home in most kitchens and is easy for any plumber to service. An older 7074300 version is marked discontinued at some retailers, and listings for different finishes give conflicting deck-thickness figures, so check the exact model and your counter thickness before ordering. Expect a simpler spray head and less refined docking than the premium picks; you are paying for a reliable valve and a recognized warranty, not features.",
    "pros": [
      "Ceramic disc valve, per retailers",
      "Limited lifetime warranty on function and finish",
      "Simple design any plumber can service",
      "Dual-spray pull-down head"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "1.5 gpm rated flow",
      "No magnetic docking or touch options",
      "Older 7074300 version discontinued"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The American Standard Colony PRO is the no-frills choice: a ceramic disc valve and a lifetime warranty at an entry-level price."
  }
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Kitchen Faucets",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-kitchen-faucet/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the best kitchen faucet brand?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Delta, Moen and Kohler are the safest choices because their parts are stocked almost everywhere and they offer lifetime limited warranties. Pfister's lifetime finish-and-function warranty is generous, and American Standard is a solid budget option. Choose a model with a ceramic cartridge and a docking system you like rather than chasing a single brand name."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Are touchless kitchen faucets worth it?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "They are worth it if you often cook with messy hands or want to keep the faucet cleaner. The trade-offs are a battery pack under the sink, occasional false triggers and a shorter warranty on the electronics, typically five years. If those bother you, a manual pull-down with good docking is simpler and lasts longer."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is 1.5 gpm enough for a kitchen faucet?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For rinsing and handwashing, 1.5 gpm is plenty. You will notice it when filling large pots, which take roughly 20% longer than at 1.8 gpm. If you fill pots often, choose 1.8 gpm, or a faucet like the Kohler Simplice with a boost button. California limits kitchen faucets to 1.8 gpm."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How long does a kitchen faucet last?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "A quality faucet with a ceramic cartridge commonly lasts 15 to 20 years, and often longer if you replace the cartridge when it wears. Spray heads, hoses and finishes may need attention sooner. Electronic parts on touch and touchless models have shorter warranties and may need replacing within their life."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can I install a kitchen faucet myself?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes, if your shut-off valves work and the sink already has the right holes. The hardest part is usually removing the old faucet's corroded nuts. A basin wrench, a flashlight and patience help. Call a plumber if you have no shut-offs, galvanized pipes or need a new hole drilled in stone."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Why does my pull-down sprayer not stay docked?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "The most common cause is a hose weight clipped in the wrong place, followed by a worn or weak docking system. Check the manual's measurement for the weight and move it as needed. Magnetic docking, such as Delta's MagnaTite or Kohler's DockNetik, is designed to hold the head in place more firmly."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Pull-down or pull-out: which is better?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Pull-down faucets suit most kitchens because their high arc clears tall pots and the head reaches into deep bowls. Pull-out faucets are better under low windows or shelves and in small sinks, and they reach the counter more easily. Measure the space above your sink before choosing."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(Astro.url.pathname),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: PRODUCTS.length,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: new URL(`#${product.id}`, Astro.url).toString(),
      })),
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: authorName(Astro.url.pathname),
    section: "Home Improvement",
    tags: [
      "best kitchen faucet",
      "pull down kitchen faucet",
      "touchless kitchen faucet",
      "kitchen faucet with sprayer",
      "best kitchen faucet brands"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <div class="absolute inset-0 opacity-10" style="background-image: radial-gradient(circle at 20% 20%, #38BDF8 0, transparent 45%), radial-gradient(circle at 80% 60%, #FF4500 0, transparent 40%);"></div>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex justify-center gap-3 mb-6">
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-[#FF4500]/20 text-[#FF4500] text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest border border-[#FF4500]/30">
            Buyer's Guide
          </span>
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-green-500/10 text-green-400 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest border border-green-500/20">
            Buyer's Guide
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          Best <span class="text-[#38BDF8]">Kitchen Faucets</span>
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-8 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
          Seven pull-down faucets from the brands plumbers actually stock, chosen on the parts that decide how a faucet feels in five years: the valve cartridge, the docking, the spray head and the warranty behind them.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 justify-center">
          <a
            href="#delta-leland-9178"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-[#FF4500] hover:bg-[#E63E00] text-white font-black rounded-xl shadow-lg transition-all no-underline"
          >
            See Our #1 Pick
          </a>
          <a
            href="#top-picks"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white font-bold rounded-xl border border-white/10 no-underline"
          >
            See the Picks
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-6 border-b border-gray-100 pb-8">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <AuthorAvatar size={48} />
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Alex Rivers</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-widest">Home Improvement Editor</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="h-10 w-px bg-gray-200 hidden sm:block"></div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block">
          <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Last Updated</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">{dates.display}</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="8.3k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#how-we-chose" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. How We Chose</a>
          <a href="#styles" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. Pull-Down, Pull-Out or Side Spray</a>
          <a href="#top-picks" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. Our 7 Picks</a>
          <a href="#touch" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. Touch and Touchless Explained</a>
          <a href="#spray" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Spray Heads and Docking</a>
          <a href="#flow" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Flow Rate: 1.5 vs 1.8 GPM</a>
          <a href="#fit" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Holes, Reach and Sink Fit</a>
          <a href="#install" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Installing a Kitchen Faucet</a>
          <a href="#warranty" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. Cartridges, Finishes and Warranty</a>
          <a href="#mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">10. Common Mistakes</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">11. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          A kitchen faucet is used more times a day than any other fixture in the house, and most of what makes one good or bad is hidden: the cartridge inside the handle, the hose and docking system inside the spout, and the warranty that decides who pays when either one fails. The finish and the shape are the easy part. This guide is about the rest.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="how-we-chose" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How We Chose
          </h2>

          <p>
            We started with the brands that plumbing supply houses and big-box stores both stock: Delta, Moen, Kohler, Pfister and American Standard. That matters more than it sounds. A faucet from a brand with a deep parts network can be fixed with a ten-dollar cartridge in fifteen minutes; a faucet from a brand nobody stocks becomes landfill when its valve wears out.
          </p>

          <p>
            From there we read spec sheets, installation manuals and trade-supplier listings for each model and compared the things that decide long-term satisfaction: the type of valve cartridge, the docking system that holds the spray head in the spout, the rated flow, the spout height and reach, the hole configuration and the warranty. We did not install or measure these faucets ourselves, and we have not invented any figure; where a number appears, it comes from the maker or a named listing.
          </p>

          <p>
            We favored pull-down faucets because that is what most people are shopping for now, and we chose at least one manual, one touch and one touchless model so the list covers every way of turning the water on. We also wanted a spread of flow rates, because the difference between 1.5 and 1.8 gallons per minute is noticeable when you fill a pasta pot.
          </p>

          <p>
            Price was a factor but not the deciding one. Every pick here sits in the range most homeowners pay for a name-brand kitchen faucet, roughly the low hundreds of dollars, and the cheapest one still carries a lifetime warranty on function and finish. We skipped unbranded marketplace faucets entirely because their warranty and parts support is impossible to verify.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are planning a larger kitchen update, our guides to the <a href="/best-dishwasher/">best dishwashers</a> and <a href="/best-under-cabinet-lighting/">best under cabinet lighting</a> cover the appliances and lighting around the sink, and the <a href="/best-under-sink-water-filter/">best under sink water filters</a> guide covers what goes in the cabinet beneath it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="styles" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Pull-Down, Pull-Out or Side Spray
          </h2>

          <p>
            A pull-down faucet has a tall, arched spout and a spray head that pulls straight down into the sink on a hose. It is the most popular style because the high arc clears tall pots and the head reaches every corner of a typical bowl. All seven picks are pull-downs.
          </p>

          <p>
            A pull-out faucet has a lower spout and a spray head that pulls toward you, more like a hand shower. It suits small sinks, kitchens with a low window or shelf above the sink, and people who want to fill a pot sitting on the counter beside the sink. The trade-off is less clearance under the spout for big pots.
          </p>

          <p>
            A faucet with a separate side sprayer uses a fixed spout and a small hose-mounted sprayer in its own deck hole. It is the oldest design and still the cheapest to replace, but the side sprayer is usually weaker and its diverter valve is a common failure point. If your sink has four holes and you like that layout, you can still buy one, or fill the extra hole with a soap dispenser.
          </p>

          <p>
            Bridge faucets and wall-mounted faucets are design choices for farmhouse and commercial-style kitchens. They look striking but need specific hole spacing or in-wall plumbing, so they are rarely a simple swap. For a straightforward replacement, a pull-down that matches your existing hole count is almost always the easiest route.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whatever style you choose, check the height above the sink against anything that hangs over it, such as a window sill, shelf or cabinet. A high-arc pull-down that cannot swing because it hits a window frame is a common and avoidable mistake.
          </p>
        </div>

