        <div id="touch" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Touch and Touchless Explained
          </h2>

          <p>
            Touch faucets, like Delta's Touch2O, sense a tap anywhere on the spout or handle and open an electronic valve. The handle still sets temperature and maximum flow; the touch simply turns the water on and off. They are genuinely useful when your hands are covered in raw chicken or bread dough, and Delta adds an automatic shut-off after four minutes in case you walk away.
          </p>

          <p>
            Touchless faucets, like Moen's MotionSense, use sensors instead. On the two-sensor Arbor, one sensor reacts to a wave over the top of the spout and another to a pot or hands placed under it. The appeal is that you never touch the faucet at all, which keeps it cleaner. The downside is occasional false triggers when you reach past the faucet for something else.
          </p>

          <p>
            Both types need power. Most run on a battery pack under the sink, typically six AA cells, and makers rate battery life in years rather than months; some models also accept an AC adapter if you have an outlet in the cabinet. When the batteries die, the faucet still works manually from the handle, so you are never left without water.
          </p>

          <p>
            Electronics shorten the warranty on that part of the faucet. Delta and Moen list lifetime limited coverage on the faucet body and finish but five years on electronic parts, so budget for the possibility of replacing a solenoid or control box later. If you want the simplest, longest-lived faucet, buy the manual version of the same model.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="spray" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Spray Heads and Docking
          </h2>

          <p>
            The spray head is where cheap and premium faucets differ most in daily use. Most offer at least two modes, an aerated stream for filling and a spray for rinsing. Better heads add a concentrated jet, such as Delta's ShieldSpray or Moen's Power Clean, or a pause button that stops the flow while you move the head between bowls.
          </p>

          <p>
            Docking is the part people complain about most after a year or two. A weak dock lets the head droop out of the spout, which looks sloppy and stresses the hose. Delta's MagnaTite and Kohler's DockNetik use a magnet to pull the head home and hold it there. Moen's Reflex system uses a weighted hose and a smooth-gliding mechanism instead, which Moen says gives secure docking.
          </p>

          <p>
            The hose matters too. Braided hoses, like Kohler's ProMotion, slide more quietly and resist kinking. Longer hoses, such as the 68-inch hose a supplier lists for the Moen Align, reach farther outside the sink, which helps when you fill a bucket or rinse a pan on the counter.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you have hard water, look for spray faces with soft rubber nozzles that you can rub clean of scale. Every faucet's spray modes weaken as mineral deposits build up in the nozzles, and a quick wipe once a month keeps the pattern even. A water softener, covered in our guide to the <a href="/best-water-softener-system/">best water softener systems</a>, prevents most of the problem at the source.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="flow" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Flow Rate: 1.5 vs 1.8 GPM
          </h2>

          <p>
            The federal limit for kitchen faucets is 2.2 gallons per minute at 60 psi, set by the Department of Energy in 1998. California's plumbing code is stricter, capping kitchen faucets at 1.8 gpm, with temporary surges to 2.2 allowed as long as the faucet returns to 1.8. Many brands now sell 1.8 gpm and 1.5 gpm models nationwide so one product meets every state's rules.
          </p>

          <p>
            WaterSense, the EPA's water-efficiency label, does not have a specification for kitchen faucets, so be wary of listings that call a kitchen faucet WaterSense certified. The EPA directs builders to the national faucet standard, ASME A112.18.1/CSA B125.1, instead.
          </p>

          <p>
            In practice, the difference between 1.5 and 1.8 gpm is noticeable mainly when you fill a large pot or a sink full of dishwater. Filling an 8-quart stockpot takes about 80 seconds at 1.5 gpm and about 67 seconds at 1.8 gpm, simple arithmetic from the rated flow. For rinsing and handwashing, you will rarely notice.
          </p>

          <p>
            Kohler's Boost button is a clever compromise: the Simplice runs at its rated 1.5 gpm normally, and Kohler says Boost raises flow by 30% when you press it. If your water pressure is low, a higher-rated faucet will help, but the rated figure assumes 60 psi, so a home at 40 psi will see less from any faucet.
          </p>

          <p>
            Lower flow does save water and the energy to heat it, which matters for people who leave the faucet running while washing dishes. If you mostly fill pots and hand-wash big pans, choose 1.8 gpm. If you mostly rinse, 1.5 gpm is fine.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="fit" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Holes, Reach and Sink Fit
          </h2>

          <p>
            Before you buy anything, look under the sink and count the holes in the sink deck or countertop. Single-hole faucets need one hole; three-hole sinks need a faucet that includes or offers an escutcheon, a deck plate that covers the outer two holes. Four-hole sinks usually had a side sprayer or soap dispenser in the fourth hole, which you can keep or cap.
          </p>

          <p>
            Spout reach is the horizontal distance from the center of the faucet to the center of the water stream. Measure from the faucet hole to the middle of your sink bowl; for a single large bowl you want the stream near the center, which often means 8 to 9 inches of reach. The Kohler Simplice lists 9 inches and the Delta Essa 9-3/8 inches, while the Moen Align is closer to 7-1/2 inches.
          </p>

          <p>
            Spout height and clearance decide whether your tallest pot fits under the faucet. Clearance is measured from the deck to the bottom of the aerator. The Delta Essa lists nearly 9-3/4 inches of clearance, which suits deep stockpots. Also check the space behind the faucet: Delta specifies at least 2-1/2 inches of clearance to the backsplash so the handle can swing fully.
          </p>

          <p>
            Deck thickness matters on stone countertops. Most faucets fit decks up to about 2-1/2 inches; the Pfister Stellen lists up to 3 inches. Thick quartz or granite with a support board underneath can exceed that, so measure before ordering.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, check the supply connections. Most modern faucets use 3/8-inch compression connections and come with flexible supply lines attached. If your shut-off valves are old and stiff, replace them at the same time; it is cheap insurance against a leak under the sink.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="install" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Installing a Kitchen Faucet
          </h2>

          <p>
            Replacing a kitchen faucet is one of the more approachable plumbing jobs, but the awkward part is working on your back inside the sink cabinet. A basin wrench, a flashlight, a bucket and towels make it far easier. Turn off both shut-off valves under the sink and open the old faucet to relieve pressure before you disconnect anything.
          </p>

          <p>
            Most of the time goes into removing the old faucet, whose mounting nuts are often corroded. Penetrating oil and patience help; cutting a stuck nut with an oscillating tool is the last resort. Clean the deck surface thoroughly so the new faucet's gasket seals flat.
          </p>

          <p>
            New pull-down faucets usually mount from above with a single nut or a quick-mount system, then connect the hot and cold supply lines and attach the spray hose to the outlet under the spout. The hose weight clips onto the hose at the point the manual specifies; set it too high or too low and the spray head will not retract or dock properly.
          </p>

          <p>
            Touch and touchless models add a control box and battery pack that mount to the cabinet wall. Plan where these go before you start, away from the garbage disposal and anywhere water might drip. If you prefer the AC adapter option, an outlet in the cabinet makes life easier than changing batteries.
          </p>

          <p>
            Flush the lines before you screw on the aerator or spray head, and check every connection for drips after the first few minutes and again the next day. If you have galvanized pipes, no shut-off valves or a sink that needs a new hole drilled in stone, a plumber is usually worth the cost. Finish the job by running a fresh bead of sealant where the sink meets the counter if the old one is cracked; our <a href="/best-bathroom-caulk/">best bathroom caulk</a> picks work just as well in the kitchen. If you want filtered drinking water from a separate tap, a whole-home option is covered in our <a href="/best-water-filtration-system-for-home/">best water filtration systems for home</a> guide.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="warranty" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Cartridges, Finishes and Warranty
          </h2>

          <p>
            The cartridge is the valve inside the handle, and it does all the work of mixing and shutting off water. Ceramic disc cartridges are now standard across reputable brands. Delta coats its discs with a diamond layer and claims they last twice the industry standard; Moen uses its own Duralast cartridges, such as the 1255 in the Align, which are stocked almost everywhere.
          </p>

          <p>
            When a faucet starts dripping or the handle gets stiff, the fix is usually a new cartridge, not a new faucet. That is where the big brands earn their price: most offer free replacement cartridges under their lifetime warranties. Kohler's warranty text, for example, says it will mail a replacement cartridge free of charge if the faucet leaks or drips during normal residential use.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finishes vary more than people expect. Chrome is the most durable and the easiest to keep clean. Stainless-look finishes, often branded spot-resistant, hide water spots well. Matte black and brushed gold look striking but can show scratches and mineral deposits sooner, and some warranties exclude certain specialty finishes, so read the fine print.
          </p>

          <p>
            Warranty terms differ by brand. Delta and Moen list lifetime limited coverage on the faucet with five years on electronics. Pfister's Pforever warranty covers finish and function for as long as you own your home. Kohler covers leaks and drips for the original buyer's ownership of the home and gives a one-year warranty for commercial use. Keep your receipt and register the faucet; it makes a future claim far easier.
          </p>

          <p>
            Cleaning matters for the finish warranty. Every maker recommends mild soap and a soft cloth and warns against abrasive cleaners, which can void finish coverage. A gentle cleaning routine keeps a premium finish looking new for years.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Not counting the holes first</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Buying a single-hole faucet for a three-hole sink without an escutcheon leaves exposed holes you then have to cover. Check under the sink before you shop, and make sure the faucet includes or offers a deck plate if you need one.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring what hangs over the sink</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A tall high-arc spout can hit a window sill, shelf or upper cabinet when it swings. Measure the vertical space above the sink and compare it with the faucet's total height before ordering, not after the box arrives.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Choosing too little reach for a big bowl</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A short spout aims water at the back of a large single bowl, so splashes land on the counter. Measure from the faucet hole to the center of the bowl and choose reach that puts the stream near the middle.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Setting the hose weight in the wrong place</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A spray head that will not retract or dock usually has its counterweight clipped in the wrong spot. Follow the manual's measurement exactly and move the weight if the head droops or sticks after installation.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Throwing out a faucet that only needs a cartridge</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A drip or stiff handle almost always means a worn cartridge. Most big brands replace cartridges free under warranty, so call the maker with your model number before buying a whole new faucet.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

