        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best kitchen faucet brand?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Delta, Moen and Kohler are the safest choices because their parts are stocked almost everywhere and they offer lifetime limited warranties. Pfister's lifetime finish-and-function warranty is generous, and American Standard is a solid budget option. Choose a model with a ceramic cartridge and a docking system you like rather than chasing a single brand name.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are touchless kitchen faucets worth it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                They are worth it if you often cook with messy hands or want to keep the faucet cleaner. The trade-offs are a battery pack under the sink, occasional false triggers and a shorter warranty on the electronics, typically five years. If those bother you, a manual pull-down with good docking is simpler and lasts longer.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is 1.5 gpm enough for a kitchen faucet?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For rinsing and handwashing, 1.5 gpm is plenty. You will notice it when filling large pots, which take roughly 20% longer than at 1.8 gpm. If you fill pots often, choose 1.8 gpm, or a faucet like the Kohler Simplice with a boost button. California limits kitchen faucets to 1.8 gpm.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does a kitchen faucet last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A quality faucet with a ceramic cartridge commonly lasts 15 to 20 years, and often longer if you replace the cartridge when it wears. Spray heads, hoses and finishes may need attention sooner. Electronic parts on touch and touchless models have shorter warranties and may need replacing within their life.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I install a kitchen faucet myself?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, if your shut-off valves work and the sink already has the right holes. The hardest part is usually removing the old faucet's corroded nuts. A basin wrench, a flashlight and patience help. Call a plumber if you have no shut-offs, galvanized pipes or need a new hole drilled in stone.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why does my pull-down sprayer not stay docked?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The most common cause is a hose weight clipped in the wrong place, followed by a worn or weak docking system. Check the manual's measurement for the weight and move it as needed. Magnetic docking, such as Delta's MagnaTite or Kohler's DockNetik, is designed to hold the head in place more firmly.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Pull-down or pull-out: which is better?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Pull-down faucets suit most kitchens because their high arc clears tall pots and the head reaches into deep bowls. Pull-out faucets are better under low windows or shelves and in small sinks, and they reach the counter more easily. Measure the space above your sink before choosing.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

